              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "The answer depends on whether you are referring to Greek mythology or modern cuisine:\n\n### 1. In Greek Mythology (Both, but traditionally **Food**)\nIn ancient Greek myths, ambrosia is most famously known as the **food of the gods**, while **nectar** is their drink. Consuming them granted the gods their immortality and prevented them from aging. \n\nHowever, the distinction wasn't always strictly kept by ancient writers:\n* **Homer\u2019s poems** (like the *Iliad* and the *Odyssey*) firmly establish **ambrosia as food** and **nectar as drink**. \n* **Other poets** (such as Sappho and Anaxandrides) flipped the roles, writing that **ambrosia was the drink** and nectar was the food.\n* **As a liquid or salve:** Ambrosia was also depicted as a liquid substance. It was used by the gods as a sweet-smelling perfume, a healing ointment for wounds, and a preservative to keep mortal bodies from decaying. Because of its honey-like consistency, it has been described as both a solid and a liquid.\n\n### 2. In Modern Cuisine (**Food**)\nIn the modern world, **ambrosia** is a **food**\u2014specifically a sweet, creamy fruit salad that originated in the United States in the late 19th century. \n\nClassic modern ambrosia salad typically includes:\n* Mandarin oranges and pineapple chunks\n* Shredded coconut\n* Miniature marshmallows\n* A creamy binder like sour cream, whipped topping (Cool Whip), or yogurt\n* Sometimes cherries, pecans, or bananas",
